{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 42, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father at a young age and was initially under the regency of his mother and Count Baldwin III of Jerusalem. He was knighted by King Amalric I in 1164 and began ruling independently in 1174 after the death of his mother.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\n**Early Reign and Captivity**\n\nRaymond IIIs early reign was marked by conflict with Nur ad Din the Zengid ruler. In 1164 he was captured at the Battle of Harim and spent the next ten years in prison. During his captivity his mother Hodierna ruled Tripoli as regent. He was released in 1174 after Nur ad Din died and he paid a large ransom. Upon his return he resumed control of Tripoli and became a key figure in the politics of the Crusader states.\n\n**Alliance with Bohemond III and the War with Saladin**\n\nRaymond III formed a close alliance with Bohemond III of Antioch. In 1180 he mediated a peace between Bohemond and the Byzantine Empire. He also arranged the marriage of his daughter to Bohemond which strengthened their bond. Raymond was known for his diplomatic skills and often acted as a mediator in disputes among the Crusader states. His alliance with Antioch brought stability to the northern Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\n**Conflict with Saladin and Later Years**\n\nRaymond III faced increasing threats from Saladin who sought to unify Muslim forces against the Crusaders. In 1182 Raymond led a campaign against Saladin but was forced to retreat. He later clashed with Guy of Lusignan the newly crowned King of Jerusalem over the succession and military strategy. Raymond opposed Guys aggressive policies and favored a more cautious approach. In 1187 he joined the disastrous Battle of Hattin where the Crusader forces were crushed by Saladin. Raymond barely escaped the battle but died later that year without a clear heir.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\n**Legacy**\n\nRaymond III was remembered as a skilled diplomat and a stabilizing force in the Crusader states. His alliances with Antioch and his cautious approach to warfare were crucial in maintaining the fragile balance of power. Despite his defeats he remained a respected figure among both Crusaders and local Christians. His death marked the end of an era in the history of the County of Tripoli and the wider Crusader states.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.3243790864944458}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.30658119916915894}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -7.986703712958843e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.03322438895702362}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -7.319182623177767e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.0132738680113107e-05}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.29521453380584717}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.19653545320034027}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00031263710116036236}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.15564532577991486}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.38928520679473877}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -7.390948667307384e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0005570290377363563}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.000590865034610033}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.4781842764932662e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00013636612857226282}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0012356749502941966}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0002335037279408425}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.9741864204406738}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0183844156563282}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -0.0002087136817863211}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.00510922959074378}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00012146688823122531}, {"token":